1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:04,464
In most prisons, when you arrive, you are given a cell. Here,

2
00:00:04,514 --> 00:00:07,151
the first question is how much money you have.

3
00:00:09,951 --> 00:00:14,676
This is San Pedro prison, in La Paz, Bolivia. From the outside,

4
00:00:14,726 --> 00:00:19,376
it looks like an old colonial building on a quiet city square.

5
00:00:19,426 --> 00:00:22,786
Pigeons. Benches. Old men reading newspapers.

6
00:00:24,586 --> 00:00:28,127
Behind its walls, there is no state housing office.

7
00:00:28,177 --> 00:00:31,647
No cell assigned by the guards. If you want a bed,

8
00:00:31,697 --> 00:00:35,308
you buy one. Or you rent one. From another prisoner.

9
00:00:38,108 --> 00:00:44,360
Inside, there are rich neighborhoods and poor ones. Estate agents. Title deeds.

10
00:00:44,410 --> 00:00:49,066
Elections. Restaurants, shops, football leagues. For years,

11
00:00:49,116 --> 00:00:53,294
there were even children living inside. And tourists,

12
00:00:53,344 --> 00:00:54,889
who paid to come in.

13
00:00:56,689 --> 00:01:01,038
The guards watch the walls. The prisoners run almost everything else.

14
00:01:01,088 --> 00:01:03,205
And in February twenty twenty six,

15
00:01:03,255 --> 00:01:09,261
Bolivia's prison authorities had to remind everyone of something that should have been obvious.

16
00:01:09,311 --> 00:01:10,982
The cells are not for sale.

17
00:01:12,782 --> 00:01:14,678
This is the story of San Pedro.

18
00:01:14,728 --> 00:01:16,812
The prison with a property market.

19
00:01:20,562 --> 00:01:22,781
Chapter 1. The panopticon.

20
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:31,792
La Paz sits in a deep valley in the Andes,

21
00:01:31,842 --> 00:01:35,794
at more than three and a half thousand meters above sea level.

22
00:01:35,844 --> 00:01:39,021
It is the highest seat of government in the world.

23
00:01:39,071 --> 00:01:42,571
Above the city rises the snow covered peak of Illimani.

24
00:01:44,371 --> 00:01:45,906
In the eighteen eighties,

25
00:01:45,956 --> 00:01:49,393
Bolivia decided to build a modern national prison here.

26
00:01:49,443 --> 00:01:51,485
According to Bolivian historians,

27
00:01:51,535 --> 00:01:57,381
construction of what was called the National Panopticon was approved in eighteen eighty five,

28
00:01:57,431 --> 00:02:00,994
with the engineer Eduardo Idiaquez in charge of the work.

29
00:02:01,044 --> 00:02:04,545
Other sources date the building to eighteen ninety five.

30
00:02:06,345 --> 00:02:10,027
The panopticon was a fashionable idea at the time.

31
00:02:10,077 --> 00:02:14,730
A prison designed so that a few guards, from one central point,

32
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:19,807
could watch every prisoner at all times. Order. Visibility. Control.

33
00:02:21,607 --> 00:02:24,852
The building went up in the San Pedro neighborhood,

34
00:02:24,902 --> 00:02:28,406
facing a city square. And that square, Plaza San Pedro,

35
00:02:28,456 --> 00:02:29,376
is still there.

36
00:02:29,426 --> 00:02:32,090
The prison is not on the edge of the city.

37
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:34,158
It is right in the middle of it.

38
00:02:35,958 --> 00:02:39,568
San Pedro was built for a few hundred prisoners.

39
00:02:39,618 --> 00:02:44,679
Sources differ on the exact number. Some say two hundred and fifty.

40
00:02:44,729 --> 00:02:50,551
Others, six hundred. Over the next century, Bolivia's prison population grew.

41
00:02:50,601 --> 00:02:52,153
The building did not.

42
00:02:53,953 --> 00:03:00,041
Reported numbers over the years range from around fifteen hundred to nearly three thousand men,

43
00:03:00,091 --> 00:03:03,594
held inside a building designed for a fraction of that.

44
00:03:03,644 --> 00:03:07,794
And somewhere along the way, the idea of total control collapsed.

45
00:03:09,594 --> 00:03:12,662
Today, according to every account of the prison,

46
00:03:12,712 --> 00:03:16,949
the police guard the perimeter. They watch the gate and the walls.

47
00:03:16,999 --> 00:03:21,106
Inside, they rarely go. The panopticon, built to see everything,

48
00:03:21,156 --> 00:03:23,120
ended up seeing almost nothing.

49
00:03:26,570 --> 00:03:29,230
Chapter 2. Welcome to San Pedro.

50
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:38,687
Imagine you have just been arrested in La Paz.

51
00:03:38,737 --> 00:03:41,432
You have not been convicted of anything.

52
00:03:41,482 --> 00:03:45,824
Like most people in Bolivian prisons, you are waiting for trial.

53
00:03:45,874 --> 00:03:48,775
That wait can last months. Sometimes years.

54
00:03:50,575 --> 00:03:52,054
As of twenty twenty six,

55
00:03:52,104 --> 00:03:56,578
more than half of Bolivia's prisoners were still waiting for a verdict.

56
00:03:56,628 --> 00:04:00,019
Older reports put the number at San Pedro even higher,

57
00:04:00,069 --> 00:04:01,421
around three quarters.

58
00:04:03,221 --> 00:04:04,699
You walk through the gate.

59
00:04:04,749 --> 00:04:08,228
And according to accounts by former inmates and journalists,

60
00:04:08,278 --> 00:04:10,639
there is usually someone waiting for you.

61
00:04:10,689 --> 00:04:13,755
A member of the reception committee. Other prisoners,

62
00:04:13,805 --> 00:04:15,284
who meet the new arrivals.

63
00:04:17,084 --> 00:04:20,033
Their first job is to explain how things work.

64
00:04:20,083 --> 00:04:24,272
And the most important thing is this. You need somewhere to live.

65
00:04:24,322 --> 00:04:26,489
The state will not give it to you.

66
00:04:28,289 --> 00:04:32,797
If you have no money, you sleep wherever you can. In corridors.

67
00:04:32,847 --> 00:04:35,329
In crowded rooms. In some sections,

68
00:04:35,379 --> 00:04:40,393
poor prisoners can stay in a small space if they work for the section,

69
00:04:40,443 --> 00:04:42,419
cleaning or running errands.

70
00:04:44,219 --> 00:04:46,258
If you have money, you go shopping.

71
00:04:50,008 --> 00:04:52,460
Chapter 3. The property market.

72
00:04:58,810 --> 00:05:01,166
San Pedro has a real estate market.

73
00:05:01,216 --> 00:05:06,184
Cells are bought and sold between prisoners. When a prisoner is released,

74
00:05:06,234 --> 00:05:08,522
transferred, or simply needs cash,

75
00:05:08,572 --> 00:05:11,065
he can sell his cell to someone else.

76
00:05:12,865 --> 00:05:15,647
According to researchers and former inmates,

77
00:05:15,697 --> 00:05:19,701
prices have ranged from about twenty dollars for a basic space,

78
00:05:19,751 --> 00:05:22,982
to around five thousand dollars for the best cells.

79
00:05:23,032 --> 00:05:24,334
In twenty twenty six,

80
00:05:24,384 --> 00:05:29,739
Bolivian journalists reported prices between three hundred and ten thousand dollars,

81
00:05:29,789 --> 00:05:32,441
or even more, in the most desirable areas.

82
00:05:34,241 --> 00:05:38,579
There are freelance estate agents. Prisoners who find buyers and sellers,

83
00:05:38,629 --> 00:05:43,027
and take a commission. You can also buy through your section's leadership,

84
00:05:43,077 --> 00:05:47,054
but according to one study, that can cost about fifty percent more.

85
00:05:48,854 --> 00:05:51,589
And there is paperwork. When a cell is sold,

86
00:05:51,639 --> 00:05:55,829
the buyer gets a title. A written document listing the room number,

87
00:05:55,879 --> 00:05:59,817
its location, a description, the previous owner, and the price.

88
00:05:59,867 --> 00:06:02,412
The sale is signed in front of a witness,

89
00:06:02,462 --> 00:06:07,222
checked by the section leader, and stamped with the section's official seal.

90
00:06:09,022 --> 00:06:10,502
None of this is legal.

91
00:06:10,552 --> 00:06:14,954
Bolivian law does not recognize any of it. But inside San Pedro,

92
00:06:15,004 --> 00:06:17,945
for decades, it worked like the real thing.

93
00:06:20,945 --> 00:06:22,208
There are also fees.

94
00:06:22,258 --> 00:06:29,363
A buyer reportedly pays a non refundable charge of twenty to twenty five percent of the price to his section.

95
00:06:29,413 --> 00:06:33,236
The money goes to maintenance, cleaning, and social events.

96
00:06:35,036 --> 00:06:38,113
If you cannot afford to buy, you can rent.

97
00:06:38,163 --> 00:06:42,655
Some prisoners own several cells and rent them out. They are,

98
00:06:42,705 --> 00:06:45,485
in effect, landlords. Inside a prison.

99
00:06:47,285 --> 00:06:50,255
In theory, a cell is also an investment.

100
00:06:50,305 --> 00:06:56,370
A prisoner who buys a good cell and improves it can sell it again when he leaves.

101
00:06:56,420 --> 00:06:59,541
The market, not the state, sets the price.

102
00:07:01,341 --> 00:07:04,344
And like any property market, location matters.

103
00:07:06,594 --> 00:07:09,022
Chapter 4. Rich and poor.

104
00:07:15,372 --> 00:07:21,273
San Pedro is divided into eight sections. Their names include Posta, Pinos,

105
00:07:21,323 --> 00:07:25,875
Alamos, San Martin, Prefectura, Palmar, Guanay and Cancha.

106
00:07:25,925 --> 00:07:30,716
Each one is like a separate neighborhood, with its own rules,

107
00:07:30,766 --> 00:07:33,572
its own leaders, and its own prices.

108
00:07:35,372 --> 00:07:38,442
At the top is La Posta. The wealthiest section.

109
00:07:38,492 --> 00:07:44,151
Cells there have been described with private bathrooms, kitchens and cable television.

110
00:07:44,201 --> 00:07:49,660
Some of the most expensive cells were reportedly extended over more than one floor.

111
00:07:49,710 --> 00:07:52,050
One account even mentions a hot tub.

112
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:58,211
The richer sections work almost like gated communities. In the evening,

113
00:07:58,261 --> 00:08:03,244
around nine o'clock, their representatives lock the doors and keep outsiders out.

114
00:08:05,044 --> 00:08:09,540
At the bottom are the poorest sections. There, the rooms are crowded.

115
00:08:09,590 --> 00:08:13,889
Five men in a single room is common. Drug addiction is widespread.

116
00:08:13,939 --> 00:08:18,435
And according to reports, that is where most of the stabbings happen,

117
00:08:18,485 --> 00:08:19,028
at night.

118
00:08:20,828 --> 00:08:24,592
The difference between the two is not the crime a man committed.

119
00:08:24,642 --> 00:08:27,035
It is not his sentence. It is his wallet.

120
00:08:30,335 --> 00:08:33,948
A drug trafficker with savings can live in relative comfort.

121
00:08:33,998 --> 00:08:39,931
A poor man waiting for trial on a minor charge can end up sleeping on the floor of a crowded room,

122
00:08:39,981 --> 00:08:42,434
in the most dangerous part of the prison.

123
00:08:44,234 --> 00:08:48,298
There is even a place inside the walls that inmates call a hotel,

124
00:08:48,348 --> 00:08:51,462
where visitors could stay the night. In San Pedro,

125
00:08:51,512 --> 00:08:53,234
almost anything has a price.

126
00:08:55,484 --> 00:08:58,574
Chapter 5. A town behind walls.

127
00:09:04,924 --> 00:09:08,496
If San Pedro has a property market, it also has a government.

128
00:09:10,296 --> 00:09:14,703
Each of the eight sections elects its own leadership every year.

129
00:09:14,753 --> 00:09:20,624
A representative. A treasurer. And secretaries for discipline, culture and education,

130
00:09:20,674 --> 00:09:24,036
sports and health. Candidates campaign for votes.

131
00:09:25,836 --> 00:09:29,793
Not everyone can run. According to one study of the prison,

132
00:09:29,843 --> 00:09:34,344
to stand for office you must have spent at least six months inside,

133
00:09:34,394 --> 00:09:38,690
own a cell that is not mortgaged, and have no outstanding debts.

134
00:09:40,490 --> 00:09:44,509
The committees collect fees, manage budgets, and keep order.

135
00:09:44,559 --> 00:09:48,645
Section leaders can send a troublemaker to an isolation cell.

136
00:09:48,695 --> 00:09:53,663
And repeat offenders can be transferred, at their request, to Chonchocoro,

137
00:09:53,713 --> 00:09:58,342
the maximum security prison outside La Paz, which every inmate fears.

138
00:10:00,142 --> 00:10:03,887
And then there is the economy. Prisoners run shops,

139
00:10:03,937 --> 00:10:09,616
restaurants and food stalls. Others work as barbers, messengers or craftsmen.

140
00:10:09,666 --> 00:10:13,262
Some sell goods made inside to the outside world.

141
00:10:15,062 --> 00:10:16,401
According to reports,

142
00:10:16,451 --> 00:10:22,221
a Bolivian beverage company once paid for the exclusive right to sell its drinks inside,

143
00:10:22,271 --> 00:10:26,255
in exchange for cash and furniture. Rival brands were banned.

144
00:10:28,055 --> 00:10:30,114
Football is taken very seriously.

145
00:10:30,164 --> 00:10:32,734
The sections play each other in a league.

146
00:10:32,784 --> 00:10:36,121
Players are reportedly bought and sold between teams.

147
00:10:36,171 --> 00:10:41,617
And betting on the matches was once estimated at up to twenty thousand dollars a year.

148
00:10:43,417 --> 00:10:48,008
Every September, on Prisoner's Day, the section leaders organize a party,

149
00:10:48,058 --> 00:10:50,042
with a barbecue and a live band.

150
00:10:51,842 --> 00:10:54,919
There is a church, and a hospital. But medical care,

151
00:10:54,969 --> 00:10:56,964
like everything else, costs money.

152
00:10:58,764 --> 00:11:01,223
It sounds almost peaceful. It was not.

153
00:11:01,273 --> 00:11:06,966
Reports from the late two thousands described an average of around four deaths a month,

154
00:11:07,016 --> 00:11:09,013
from illness and from violence.

155
00:11:09,063 --> 00:11:13,898
Men who hurt women or children were punished harshly by the other inmates.

156
00:11:13,948 --> 00:11:16,011
Some reportedly did not survive.

157
00:11:17,811 --> 00:11:21,949
San Pedro is not unique in this. Across Bolivia's prison system,

158
00:11:21,999 --> 00:11:26,989
the official description is that the authorities control the outer perimeter,

159
00:11:27,039 --> 00:11:30,588
and that inside, prisoners largely organize themselves.

160
00:11:30,638 --> 00:11:33,468
San Pedro is simply the most famous example.

161
00:11:35,718 --> 00:11:38,448
Chapter 6. Children inside.

162
00:11:44,798 --> 00:11:50,342
There was one more thing that made San Pedro different from almost any prison in the world.

163
00:11:50,392 --> 00:11:50,895
Families.

164
00:11:52,695 --> 00:11:55,844
In Bolivia, many prisoners' families are poor.

165
00:11:55,894 --> 00:11:57,861
When a father goes to prison,

166
00:11:57,911 --> 00:12:01,268
his wife and children can lose their only income,

167
00:12:01,318 --> 00:12:04,120
and sometimes their home. So for decades,

168
00:12:04,170 --> 00:12:07,249
Bolivian prisons allowed families to move in.

169
00:12:09,049 --> 00:12:12,704
Wives and children lived inside San Pedro with the prisoners.

170
00:12:12,754 --> 00:12:13,615
In the morning,

171
00:12:13,665 --> 00:12:17,563
children walked out through the gate to go to school in the city.

172
00:12:17,613 --> 00:12:20,478
In the afternoon, they came back. To the prison.

173
00:12:20,528 --> 00:12:26,006
Reporting from around two thousand eight described about two hundred children living there.

174
00:12:27,806 --> 00:12:31,338
For some families, it was the only way to stay together.

175
00:12:31,388 --> 00:12:35,241
For others, it meant children growing up surrounded by drugs,

176
00:12:35,291 --> 00:12:36,584
alcohol and violence.

177
00:12:38,384 --> 00:12:42,345
In June twenty thirteen, the system became a national scandal.

178
00:12:42,395 --> 00:12:47,519
Bolivian officials said a twelve year old girl living in San Pedro was pregnant,

179
00:12:47,569 --> 00:12:51,465
after being abused for years by relatives held in the prison.

180
00:12:51,515 --> 00:12:54,958
Officials later gave conflicting accounts of the case.

181
00:12:55,008 --> 00:12:57,286
But the public outrage was enormous.

182
00:13:00,286 --> 00:13:06,131
The government announced it would remove children over the age of six from the country's prisons.

183
00:13:06,181 --> 00:13:10,567
Prisoners at San Pedro protested, partly against plans to close the jail.

184
00:13:10,617 --> 00:13:11,296
At the time,

185
00:13:11,346 --> 00:13:17,859
officials estimated that around fifteen hundred children were living in Bolivian prisons with their parents.

186
00:13:19,659 --> 00:13:22,434
Moving them out proved slow and difficult.

187
00:13:22,484 --> 00:13:25,797
Many had nowhere else to go. Under Bolivian rules,

188
00:13:25,847 --> 00:13:30,034
young children could still stay with a parent. And in practice,

189
00:13:30,084 --> 00:13:33,800
according to later reports, some older children did too.

190
00:13:36,050 --> 00:13:38,560
Chapter 7. The tourist prison.

191
00:13:44,910 --> 00:13:46,426
In nineteen ninety six,

192
00:13:46,476 --> 00:13:50,851
a young man named Thomas McFadden was arrested at La Paz airport,

193
00:13:50,901 --> 00:13:54,731
carrying about five kilograms of cocaine. He was British,

194
00:13:54,781 --> 00:13:56,706
and he was sent to San Pedro.

195
00:13:56,756 --> 00:13:59,905
He was sentenced to six years and eight months.

196
00:14:01,705 --> 00:14:04,446
McFadden had no money for a good cell.

197
00:14:04,496 --> 00:14:06,135
So he found a business.

198
00:14:06,185 --> 00:14:11,203
He started giving guided tours of the prison. To foreign backpackers.

199
00:14:13,003 --> 00:14:16,726
Word spread. Travelers passed his name to each other.

200
00:14:16,776 --> 00:14:21,069
And guidebooks mentioned it. Tourists would come to the gate,

201
00:14:21,119 --> 00:14:26,124
pay the guards, and walk inside, where McFadden would show them around.

202
00:14:26,174 --> 00:14:30,254
The kitchens. The cells. The sections. The football pitch.

203
00:14:32,054 --> 00:14:36,809
According to later reports, a tour could cost around four hundred bolivianos,

204
00:14:36,859 --> 00:14:41,489
about fifty seven dollars. Visitors were searched, told not to take photos,

205
00:14:41,539 --> 00:14:44,235
and told to leave before six in the evening.

206
00:14:44,285 --> 00:14:48,292
Some reportedly stayed overnight, in what inmates called a hotel.

207
00:14:50,092 --> 00:14:53,113
And many of the tours came with something extra.

208
00:14:53,163 --> 00:14:57,912
According to reporting at the time, cocaine was produced inside the prison,

209
00:14:57,962 --> 00:15:01,431
in large laboratories and in crude setups in the cells.

210
00:15:01,481 --> 00:15:03,926
And some was sold to visiting tourists.

211
00:15:05,726 --> 00:15:12,201
One of the backpackers who heard about McFadden was a young Australian law graduate named Rusty Young.

212
00:15:12,251 --> 00:15:14,503
He took the tour. Then he came back.

213
00:15:14,553 --> 00:15:16,486
He bribed his way into staying,

214
00:15:16,536 --> 00:15:21,284
and lived inside San Pedro for about three months, sharing McFadden's cell.

215
00:15:23,084 --> 00:15:26,211
McFadden was released around the year two thousand.

216
00:15:26,261 --> 00:15:29,950
Young finished writing his story, and in two thousand three,

217
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,065
it was published as a book called Marching Powder.

218
00:15:33,115 --> 00:15:35,432
It became an international bestseller.

219
00:15:37,232 --> 00:15:41,445
The book made San Pedro famous. Backpackers came looking for it.

220
00:15:41,495 --> 00:15:42,244
At its peak,

221
00:15:42,294 --> 00:15:45,973
some estimates said there were around fifty tours a day.

222
00:15:46,023 --> 00:15:50,835
Hollywood bought the film rights. Actors were announced to play McFadden.

223
00:15:50,885 --> 00:15:53,033
The film has still not been made.

224
00:15:55,833 --> 00:16:00,601
For the Bolivian government, it was an embarrassment. In two thousand nine,

225
00:16:00,651 --> 00:16:04,391
a tourist's video from inside the prison was posted online,

226
00:16:04,441 --> 00:16:07,218
and Bolivian television picked up the story.

227
00:16:07,268 --> 00:16:10,109
The authorities moved to shut the tours down.

228
00:16:11,909 --> 00:16:16,996
Bolivian newspapers reported that the police managed to reduce prison tourism,

229
00:16:17,046 --> 00:16:20,421
but not to stop it completely. For years afterwards,

230
00:16:20,471 --> 00:16:23,253
travelers still told stories of getting in.

231
00:16:25,053 --> 00:16:28,568
Marching Powder is still sold in bookshops around the world.

232
00:16:28,618 --> 00:16:29,578
For many readers,

233
00:16:29,628 --> 00:16:33,855
it is the first time they hear that a prison like this can exist at all.

234
00:16:36,105 --> 00:16:38,882
Chapter 8. The cells are not for sale.

235
00:16:45,232 --> 00:16:49,477
The tours faded. The children were moved out, at least officially.

236
00:16:49,527 --> 00:16:51,624
But the property market survived.

237
00:16:53,424 --> 00:16:58,250
In two thousand nineteen, a former mayor of El Alto, Edgar Patana,

238
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:02,165
held in San Pedro, was transferred to another prison.

239
00:17:02,215 --> 00:17:07,558
According to Bolivian press reports, he had been accused of deals inside,

240
00:17:07,608 --> 00:17:11,178
including promoting cell rentals and renovations.

241
00:17:12,978 --> 00:17:17,040
And the problem was not only San Pedro. In other Bolivian prisons,

242
00:17:17,090 --> 00:17:21,837
the national ombudsman found that many young inmates had to pay an entry fee,

243
00:17:21,887 --> 00:17:23,830
known as the right to the floor,

244
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:27,132
of between seven hundred and two thousand bolivianos.

245
00:17:27,182 --> 00:17:30,434
Those who could not pay were sent to the worst areas.

246
00:17:32,234 --> 00:17:35,666
The country's largest prison, Palmasola, in Santa Cruz,

247
00:17:35,716 --> 00:17:39,085
works in a similar way, and has been far more violent.

248
00:17:39,135 --> 00:17:40,731
In August twenty thirteen,

249
00:17:40,781 --> 00:17:45,668
a battle between rival groups of inmates there left around thirty people dead,

250
00:17:45,718 --> 00:17:46,934
many of them burned.

251
00:17:48,734 --> 00:17:50,855
And the violence has not gone away.

252
00:17:50,905 --> 00:17:53,769
In the first seven months of twenty twenty six,

253
00:17:53,819 --> 00:17:58,606
Bolivia's ombudsman counted seventeen violent deaths in the country's prisons.

254
00:17:58,656 --> 00:18:01,210
More than in the whole of the year before.

255
00:18:03,010 --> 00:18:05,764
Meanwhile, Bolivia's prisons kept filling up.

256
00:18:05,814 --> 00:18:07,821
By the end of twenty twenty five,

257
00:18:07,871 --> 00:18:10,749
they held more than thirty two thousand people.

258
00:18:10,799 --> 00:18:12,930
At one point in twenty twenty five,

259
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:17,541
the system was reported to be more than one hundred percent over capacity.

260
00:18:19,341 --> 00:18:24,013
On February twenty sixth, twenty twenty six, Colonel Daniel Merida,

261
00:18:24,063 --> 00:18:26,762
Bolivia's national director of prisons,

262
00:18:26,812 --> 00:18:30,285
gave an interview to the Bolivian news agency ANF.

263
00:18:30,335 --> 00:18:31,836
His message was blunt.

264
00:18:33,636 --> 00:18:38,074
Prisoners' investments in building or improving their cells, he said,

265
00:18:38,124 --> 00:18:40,286
do not create any property rights.

266
00:18:40,336 --> 00:18:43,343
The land and the buildings belong to the state.

267
00:18:45,143 --> 00:18:48,144
Any improvement or construction they make, he said,

268
00:18:48,194 --> 00:18:50,956
is at their own risk, and gives them no rights.

269
00:18:53,956 --> 00:18:59,181
He said the state could reorganize the spaces whenever it considered it necessary,

270
00:18:59,231 --> 00:19:01,755
including moving inmates to other wings.

271
00:19:01,805 --> 00:19:04,199
He said strict orders had been issued,

272
00:19:04,249 --> 00:19:06,515
banning charges for access to cells.

273
00:19:06,565 --> 00:19:12,562
And that cameras would help identify prisoners and police officers involved in irregularities.

274
00:19:14,362 --> 00:19:16,767
In other words, after more than a century,

275
00:19:16,817 --> 00:19:19,514
the state was trying to take back the building.

276
00:19:22,514 --> 00:19:26,439
Whether that is possible is another question. For generations,

277
00:19:26,489 --> 00:19:30,286
prisoners in San Pedro have paid real money for their cells.

278
00:19:30,336 --> 00:19:34,133
Some have spent their savings. Some have borrowed. For them,

279
00:19:34,183 --> 00:19:36,441
a cell is not just a place to sleep.

280
00:19:36,491 --> 00:19:38,941
It is the most valuable thing they own.

281
00:19:41,191 --> 00:19:44,188
Chapter 9. Why it still exists.

282
00:19:50,538 --> 00:19:53,542
So why has San Pedro not simply been closed?

283
00:19:55,342 --> 00:19:58,533
There have been plans. In the mid two thousands,

284
00:19:58,583 --> 00:20:01,910
during the presidency of Eduardo Rodriguez Veltze,

285
00:20:01,960 --> 00:20:06,300
a project was prepared to move the prison out of the city center,

286
00:20:06,350 --> 00:20:09,879
to a site in the south of La Paz. In twenty thirteen,

287
00:20:09,929 --> 00:20:14,607
after the child abuse scandal, closure was discussed again. Each time,

288
00:20:14,657 --> 00:20:15,822
the prison stayed.

289
00:20:17,622 --> 00:20:19,456
Part of the reason is simple.

290
00:20:19,506 --> 00:20:22,184
Bolivia does not have enough prison space.

291
00:20:22,234 --> 00:20:27,444
Closing San Pedro would mean finding somewhere else for well over a thousand men,

292
00:20:27,494 --> 00:20:29,588
in a system that is already full.

293
00:20:31,388 --> 00:20:35,019
Part of the reason is that the system, in a strange way,

294
00:20:35,069 --> 00:20:38,964
works. A state with little money for prisons has, in effect,

295
00:20:39,014 --> 00:20:42,908
let the prisoners pay for their own housing, their own food,

296
00:20:42,958 --> 00:20:45,867
their own order. The guards control the gate.

297
00:20:45,917 --> 00:20:47,445
The inmates do the rest.

298
00:20:49,245 --> 00:20:52,864
And that is exactly what critics say is wrong with it.

299
00:20:52,914 --> 00:20:53,747
In San Pedro,

300
00:20:53,797 --> 00:20:57,144
the price of justice is not the same for everyone.

301
00:20:57,194 --> 00:21:00,065
A man's safety, his space, even his health,

302
00:21:00,115 --> 00:21:03,327
depend on how much money he has. Poor prisoners,

303
00:21:03,377 --> 00:21:06,927
many of them never convicted, end up paying the most.

304
00:21:08,727 --> 00:21:12,061
Today, the building still stands on Plaza San Pedro.

305
00:21:12,111 --> 00:21:14,403
Tourists sit on the benches outside.

306
00:21:14,453 --> 00:21:16,941
Some of them have read Marching Powder.

307
00:21:16,991 --> 00:21:20,585
They look at the white walls, and the heavy wooden door.

308
00:21:20,635 --> 00:21:23,188
And they wonder what is going on inside.

309
00:21:24,988 --> 00:21:29,233
Somewhere behind those walls, someone is probably selling a cell right now.

310
00:21:32,533 --> 00:21:35,638
So what do you think? Is San Pedro a disgrace,

311
00:21:35,688 --> 00:21:38,587
or a practical solution for a poor country?

312
00:21:38,637 --> 00:21:40,233
Tell us in the comments.

313
00:21:40,283 --> 00:21:46,200
And if you want to see what happens when a prison economy turns into a criminal empire,

314
00:21:46,250 --> 00:21:49,218
watch our story about Tocoron, in Venezuela.
